284
00:09:43,860 --> 00:09:44,240
It cannot,

285
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:45,720
it won't go directly and...

286
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:46,920
for this...

287
00:09:46,920 --> 00:09:48,980
that is, take data from memory

288
00:09:48,980 --> 00:09:50,260
and directly operate on it.

289
00:09:50,260 --> 00:09:51,200
Before it can compute,

290
00:09:51,200 --> 00:09:51,740
it can only...

291
00:09:51,740 --> 00:09:52,860
if the data is outside,

292
00:09:52,860 --> 00:09:53,960
it can only load it from memory

293
00:09:53,960 --> 00:09:54,820
first into what?

294
00:09:54,820 --> 00:09:55,740
Into the registers.

295
00:09:55,740 --> 00:09:57,660
Only then can its processing unit

296
00:09:57,660 --> 00:09:59,200
take the data from the registers

297
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:00,160
and process it.

298
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:01,260
After the data is processed,

299
00:10:01,260 --> 00:10:03,060
it is also placed into the registers first.

300
00:10:03,060 --> 00:10:03,800
Do you all understand what I mean?

301
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:05,900
And if you want to save it,

302
00:10:05,900 --> 00:10:06,880
it can only be moved out

303
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:07,620
from the registers

304
00:10:07,620 --> 00:10:09,280
and put somewhere else.

305
00:10:09,280 --> 00:10:11,220
So when we...

306
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:12,520
the instructions for these operations,

307
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:13,000
it's the same story.

308
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:13,920
In the instructions you work with,

309
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:14,840
when you perform operations,

310
00:10:14,840 --> 00:10:16,160
if you want to do arithmetic or logic,

311
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:17,900
addition, subtraction, multiplication, division,

312
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:18,960
you can only

313
00:10:18,960 --> 00:10:20,420
fetch data from the registers,

314
00:10:20,420 --> 00:10:21,040
and then...

315
00:10:21,040 --> 00:10:22,840
the result can only go back into the registers.

316
00:10:22,840 --> 00:10:23,480
That's what it means.

317
00:10:23,480 --> 00:10:28,640
So that's the stuff related to registers.

318
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:32,080
Now, besides operating on registers,

319
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:34,480
our instructions also need to be able to operate on memory,

320
00:10:34,480 --> 00:10:35,460
because, as I just mentioned,

321
00:10:35,460 --> 00:10:38,380
Your data ultimately still has to be fetched in from outside.

322
00:10:38,380 --> 00:10:41,460
You have to move the data from memory into the registers first.

323
00:10:41,460 --> 00:10:44,120
So for moving data back and forth between registers and memory,

324
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:46,200
for these read, write, and move operations,

325
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:47,860
there are also some special instructions to handle them.

326
00:10:47,860 --> 00:10:50,300
So memory is also something that our instructions

327
00:10:50,300 --> 00:10:54,340
operate on as a basic object.

328
00:10:54,340 --> 00:10:56,960
Now, when it comes to operating on memory,

329
00:10:56,960 --> 00:10:58,360
there are a few concepts you should pay attention to.

330
00:10:58,360 --> 00:11:03,920
That is, for read and write operations,

331
00:11:03,920 --> 00:11:04,880
for accesses,

332
00:11:04,880 --> 00:11:06,720
what is the basic unit?

333
00:11:06,720 --> 00:11:07,260
It's the byte.

334
00:11:07,260 --> 00:11:09,340
This concept should be very simple.

335
00:11:09,340 --> 00:11:11,880
That is, when we read data,

336
00:11:11,880 --> 00:11:14,680
we read it one byte at a time.

337
00:11:14,680 --> 00:11:17,260
Each byte

338
00:11:17,260 --> 00:11:18,640
actually implies a concept,

339
00:11:18,640 --> 00:11:20,260
which is that every byte has an address.

340
00:11:20,260 --> 00:11:23,640
That is, when we've been learning high-level languages,

341
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:25,740
we keep saying a pointer points to that address

342
00:11:25,740 --> 00:11:27,200
now, strictly speaking, that address

343
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:29,840
corresponds to the concept of a single byte

344
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,080
every byte has an address

345
00:11:31,080 --> 00:11:32,060
every byte has an address

346
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:32,360
right?

347
00:11:32,360 --> 00:11:33,480
that's what it means

348
00:11:33,480 --> 00:11:37,820
so for our RV32,

349
00:11:37,820 --> 00:11:41,000
that is, for our 32-bit machine,

350
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,260
the memory space it can access,

351
00:11:43,260 --> 00:11:46,400
how many bytes can it access at most?

352
00:11:46,400 --> 00:11:48,460
how many bytes can it access?

353
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:50,360
it's 2 to the power of 32

354
00:11:50,360 --> 00:11:52,620
and if you convert that,

355
00:11:52,620 --> 00:11:53,200
that's 4G

356
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:56,340
so this is the basic concept

357
00:11:56,340 --> 00:11:58,820
of operands

358
00:11:58,820 --> 00:12:01,600
what we just talked about was

359
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:03,240
the assembly,

360
00:12:03,240 --> 00:12:05,320
the assembly instructions we operate on in RISC-V

361
00:12:05,320 --> 00:12:06,280
some basic objects.

362
00:12:06,280 --> 00:12:08,580
Now, next, let's take a look

363
00:12:08,580 --> 00:12:09,960
at assembly instructions,

364
00:12:09,960 --> 00:12:12,340
the concept of their so-called encoding format.

365
00:12:12,340 --> 00:12:14,980
Now, this concept of encoding format

366
00:12:14,980 --> 00:12:16,820
actually, earlier on,

367
00:12:16,820 --> 00:12:22,500
when I gave you some introductory overview,

368
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:25,640
talking about the mini computer,

369
00:12:25,640 --> 00:12:28,060
when we designed an assembly instruction ourselves,

370
00:12:28,060 --> 00:12:30,340
we actually mentioned this concept back then too.

371
00:12:30,340 --> 00:12:31,640
Think back for a moment.

372
00:12:31,640 --> 00:12:34,680
Remember when we got to that page,

373
00:12:34,680 --> 00:12:36,580
the assembly instruction we designed,

374
00:12:36,580 --> 00:12:38,460
we even said at the time,

375
00:12:38,460 --> 00:12:40,160
just for show,

376
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:41,940
we actually came up with two instruction formats.

377
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:45,220
Actually, that is related to this concept here.

378
00:12:45,220 --> 00:12:46,820
That is to say, these

379
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:48,160
assembly instructions of ours,

380
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:49,520
because we know that for assembly instructions,

381
00:12:49,520 --> 00:12:51,400
It ultimately still corresponds to

382
00:12:51,400 --> 00:12:52,840
being translated into those machine instructions.

383
00:12:52,840 --> 00:12:53,960
Now, inside that machine instruction,

384
00:12:53,960 --> 00:12:54,920
every single

385
00:12:54,920 --> 00:12:56,700
32-bit word,

386
00:12:56,700 --> 00:12:57,840
those bytes,

387
00:12:57,840 --> 00:12:58,660
those contents,

388
00:12:58,660 --> 00:13:00,640
actually carry different meanings.

389
00:13:00,640 --> 00:13:01,060
So let's

390
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:03,700
go over the basic concepts once more.

391
00:13:03,700 --> 00:13:04,640
Next,

392
00:13:04,640 --> 00:13:05,500
we're going to

393
00:13:05,500 --> 00:13:07,720
take a closer look at this.

394
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:08,320
So here,

395
00:13:08,320 --> 00:13:10,180
for RISC-V,

396
00:13:10,180 --> 00:13:11,820
we mainly mean this

397
00:13:11,820 --> 00:13:13,500
basic

398
00:13:13,500 --> 00:13:14,640
integer instruction set,

399
00:13:14,640 --> 00:13:18,620
it has six encoding formats in total,

400
00:13:18,620 --> 00:13:22,720
called the R, I, S, B, U, and J types respectively.

401
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:23,640
What exactly it is,

402
00:13:23,640 --> 00:13:25,500
we'll take a look when we get there.

403
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:27,400
So overall, we can see

404
00:13:27,400 --> 00:13:28,860
what kinds of concepts are involved here.

405
00:13:28,860 --> 00:13:32,300
First, this involves the question of instruction length.

406
00:13:32,300 --> 00:13:35,180
In the specification,

407
00:13:35,180 --> 00:13:36,820
there is a technical term

408
00:13:36,820 --> 00:13:39,780
called capital-I ILEN1,

409
00:13:39,780 --> 00:13:43,300
a name like a variable.

410
00:13:43,300 --> 00:13:45,540
You can look it up; it's in the specification.

411
00:13:45,540 --> 00:13:47,500
It is actually used to refer to

412
00:13:47,500 --> 00:13:49,760
the length of the instructions

413
00:13:49,760 --> 00:13:52,440
under different processors.

414
00:13:52,440 --> 00:13:53,420
So for now,

415
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:54,420
that is to say,

416
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:55,720
for us,

417
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:58,440
we mainly target RV32I,

418
00:13:58,440 --> 00:14:02,140
which is to say, for 32-bit machines,

419
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:03,760
the length of its instructions

420
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:06,220
is 32 bits.

421
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:06,760
So what does that mean?

422
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:08,420
It means how big one instruction is.

423
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:10,080
We usually tend to use bytes.

424
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:11,860
So 32 bits is how many bytes?

425
00:14:11,860 --> 00:14:13,260
That's 4 bytes.

426
00:14:13,260 --> 00:14:13,940
Right?

427
00:14:13,940 --> 00:14:15,800
So one instruction is just 4 bytes.

428
00:14:15,800 --> 00:14:18,380
So that's actually quite convenient for us to parse.

429
00:14:18,380 --> 00:14:22,480
I mean, once you get a machine instruction file,

430
00:14:22,480 --> 00:14:23,820
say you're asked to do disassembly,

431
00:14:23,820 --> 00:14:26,700
how do you actually pull out the instructions one by one?

432
00:14:26,700 --> 00:14:28,200
You just start from the beginning,

433
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:29,420
4 bytes at a time,

434
00:14:29,420 --> 00:14:30,640
take them out 4 bytes at a time.

435
00:14:30,640 --> 00:14:32,640
That's basically the idea.

436
00:14:32,640 --> 00:14:33,760
Now this...

437
00:14:33,760 --> 00:14:37,900
But some cases may not be like that.

438
00:14:37,900 --> 00:14:39,580
For instance, when the compressed extension is supported,

439
00:14:39,580 --> 00:14:41,360
an instruction may be just 16 bytes --

440
00:14:41,360 --> 00:14:42,400
I mean 16 bits.

441
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:43,620
That is, half.

442
00:14:43,620 --> 00:14:44,760
So under this situation,

443
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:47,140
our course will not discuss it.

444
00:14:47,140 --> 00:14:48,700
Because what we mainly talk about is

445
00:14:48,700 --> 00:14:50,060
the uncompressed case.

446
00:14:50,060 --> 00:14:52,960
Now the second concept-related point,

447
00:14:52,960 --> 00:14:54,260
that is the issue of instruction alignment.

448
00:14:54,260 --> 00:14:56,320
What do we mean by the issue of instruction alignment?

449
00:14:56,320 --> 00:14:58,460
Well, we know that this instruction,

450
00:14:58,460 --> 00:15:00,240
normally it sits on the hard drive as a file,

451
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:01,280
and when it is executed,

452
00:15:01,280 --> 00:15:03,140
we need to take it from the hard drive and do what with it?

453
00:15:03,140 --> 00:15:04,900
First, as the first step, read it to where first?

454
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:06,920
First load it into memory.

455
00:15:06,920 --> 00:15:07,140
Right?

456
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:09,300
So the so-called issue of alignment,

457
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:11,320
actually refers to, inside memory,

458
00:15:11,320 --> 00:15:12,280
this machine instruction,

459
00:15:12,280 --> 00:15:17,540
the address value of the position where it is placed,

460
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:18,300
right?

461
00:15:18,300 --> 00:15:21,560
For example, let's say it's 32-bit aligned.

462
00:15:21,560 --> 00:15:23,280
Then its size is four bytes.

463
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:25,720
Its address is always a multiple of four bytes.

464
00:15:25,720 --> 00:15:29,300
Its address is definitely 4, 8, 12,

465
00:15:29,300 --> 00:15:30,160
something like that.

466
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:31,120
You get what I mean?

467
00:15:31,120 --> 00:15:32,220
That's what we call alignment.

468
00:15:32,220 --> 00:15:33,520
If things get misaligned,

469
00:15:33,520 --> 00:15:35,060
it may cause the program to behave abnormally.

470
00:15:35,060 --> 00:15:38,140
So this is actually specified in the specification too.

471
00:15:38,140 --> 00:15:39,300
That is, when you load it,

472
00:15:39,300 --> 00:15:41,500
you must load it into memory at an aligned address.

473
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:43,760
The so-called instruction alignment is this concept.

474
00:15:43,760 --> 00:15:44,480
That is, it's loaded at an address where...

475
00:15:44,480 --> 00:15:46,460
The so-called alignment here is 32 bits.

476
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:48,380
In practice, that means the address it's loaded to

477
00:15:48,380 --> 00:15:49,980
must be a multiple of 4.

478
00:15:49,980 --> 00:15:52,340
Of course, we probably don't care much about this process.

479
00:15:52,340 --> 00:15:54,220
This is probably handled when the program runs,

480
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:55,860
and for us, it's really just hitting the Enter key.

481
00:15:55,860 --> 00:15:57,940
But in this process, the loading process,

482
00:15:57,940 --> 00:16:00,520
is actually done for you by the system.

483
00:16:00,520 --> 00:16:04,040
That's the second concept.

484
00:16:04,040 --> 00:16:05,540
Now the third one is,

485
00:16:05,540 --> 00:16:07,320
when we talk about an instruction,

486
00:16:07,320 --> 00:16:08,640
what you get is four bytes,

487
00:16:08,640 --> 00:16:10,240
and those four bytes are 32 bits.

488
00:16:10,240 --> 00:16:11,600
So these are bits,

489
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:14,040
and those 32 bits actually carry meaning.

490
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:15,800
So we take these 32 bits,

491
00:16:15,800 --> 00:16:18,580
and depending on the encoding format of the instruction,

492
00:16:18,580 --> 00:16:19,640
we divide them into different fields.

493
00:16:19,640 --> 00:16:21,320
As you can see up here, look,

494
00:16:21,320 --> 00:16:22,640
this is a table, right?

495
00:16:22,640 --> 00:16:24,220
For example, this one,

496
00:16:24,220 --> 00:16:25,340
for example, we have,

497
00:16:25,340 --> 00:16:27,860
this R-type,

498
00:16:27,860 --> 00:16:28,700
for the R-type,

499
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:30,600
this is basically like,

500
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:31,620
that's one instruction.

501
00:16:31,620 --> 00:16:33,780
So for this instruction

502
00:16:33,780 --> 00:16:36,000
we can see it's split into many parts

503
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,180
divided into many parts like this

504
00:16:38,180 --> 00:16:39,960
this is the concept of fields

505
00:16:39,960 --> 00:16:42,000
each part is called a field

506
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,620
each field has a different meaning

507
00:16:44,620 --> 00:16:47,100
we'll look at this carefully later

508
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:48,480
when we cover each instruction format

509
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:51,440
we'll talk about what it means

510
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:52,680
what exactly this thing is

511
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:54,320
why do we need to understand this?

512
00:16:54,320 --> 00:16:56,300
Well, it's because we sometimes need to look things up in the manual

513
00:16:56,300 --> 00:16:56,760
we need to look things up

514
00:16:56,760 --> 00:16:58,220
For example, when you get

515
00:16:58,220 --> 00:17:00,100
because in our low-level development

516
00:17:00,100 --> 00:17:01,140
we often have to deal with

517
00:17:01,140 --> 00:17:04,140
analyzing situations in memory

518
00:17:04,140 --> 00:17:06,600
Unlike high-level programmers, who may not care much about this

519
00:17:06,600 --> 00:17:08,380
high-level programmers are probably more focused on

520
00:17:08,380 --> 00:17:09,420
business logic issues

521
00:17:09,420 --> 00:17:11,780
But in low-level development, we tend to focus more on

522
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:13,840
the hardware, for example, some things in memory

523
00:17:13,840 --> 00:17:16,260
that we dig into bit by bit,

524
00:17:16,260 --> 00:17:18,100
and sometimes you run into this kind of situation.

525
00:17:18,100 --> 00:17:20,620
So just try to get a feel for

526
00:17:20,620 --> 00:17:23,220
how this differs from developing in high-level languages.

527
00:17:23,220 --> 00:17:26,920
Now, since we're going to dig into the state in memory,

528
00:17:26,920 --> 00:17:28,880
you definitely need to know

529
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:30,740
what each field in memory actually is.

530
00:17:30,740 --> 00:17:33,740
As for these specific fields

531
00:17:33,740 --> 00:17:35,720
and exactly what they mean,

532
00:17:35,720 --> 00:17:37,160
of course, that's all in the manual,

533
00:17:37,160 --> 00:17:38,200
and we'll take a look at it together in a bit.

534
00:17:38,200 --> 00:17:39,400
So first, we know

535
00:17:39,400 --> 00:17:40,940
it's divided into different fields,

536
00:17:40,940 --> 00:17:43,320
and for each instruction,

537
00:17:43,320 --> 00:17:44,120
exactly, for example,

538
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:45,760
is it used for addition,

539
00:17:45,760 --> 00:17:47,180
or is it used for subtraction,

540
00:17:47,180 --> 00:17:48,440
or is it used for something else?

541
00:17:48,440 --> 00:17:50,260
So that's the concept of instruction types.

542
00:17:50,260 --> 00:17:52,040
Now, about these instruction types,

543
00:17:52,040 --> 00:17:53,860
let's actually mention one thing,

544
00:17:53,860 --> 00:17:55,900
which is that this opcode field alone

545
00:17:55,900 --> 00:17:57,880
cannot uniquely determine

546
00:17:57,880 --> 00:18:00,480
the type of this instruction.

547
00:18:00,480 --> 00:18:02,060
This is probably a bit different

548
00:18:02,060 --> 00:18:02,840
from the concept we had back then

549
00:18:02,840 --> 00:18:04,380
in that simple

550
00:18:04,380 --> 00:18:05,440
mini computer design.

551
00:18:05,440 --> 00:18:06,600
There's a slight difference.

552
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:07,940
This opcode field,

553
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:09,480
this opcode thing,

554
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:10,700
cannot uniquely determine

555
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:12,240
whether this is an add instruction

556
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:14,500
or some kind of subtraction instruction.

557
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:16,840
In fact, it's determined through

558
00:18:16,840 --> 00:18:18,000
the opcode and

559
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,140
what was mentioned here just now,

560
00:18:20,140 --> 00:18:21,300
function3,

561
00:18:21,300 --> 00:18:21,700
function

562
00:18:21,700 --> 00:18:23,420
this funct

563
00:18:23,420 --> 00:18:25,240
These are all terms from the specification.

564
00:18:25,240 --> 00:18:25,960
I copied them out.

565
00:18:25,960 --> 00:18:27,120
